{"config":{"lang":["en"],"separator":"[\\s\\-]+","pipeline":["stopWordFilter"]},"docs":[{"location":"","title":"Home","text":"<p> I'm a Physics PhD candidate at McGill University and the Trottier Space Institute. I'm interested in all things gravitational-waves, whether they be from mergers of black holes and neutrons stars, core-collapse supernovae, or spinning pulsars!</p> <p>My current research involves using machine learning techniques to make rapid predictions for LIGO-Virgo candidate gravitational-wave events. On this website you can read more about my research, including info on projects I work on, and some more info about me.</p> <p>Email: nayyer.raza@mail.mcgill.ca</p> <p>ADS: Nayyer Raza</p> <p>GitHub: nayyer-raza</p>"},{"location":"#research-interests","title":"Research Interests","text":"<p>I work at the intersection of machine learning and gravitational-wave astronomy. The type of astrophysical sources I study are core-collapse supernovae and mergers of dense compact objects like neutron stars and black holes. I've worked extensively with neural networks to build models that can enable the study of these sources, and am particularly interested in the interpretably of these ML models.</p> <p></p> <p>Animation credits: NASA/Goddard/CI Lab</p> <p></p> <p>Some recent papers:</p> <ul> <li>GWSkyNet-Multi II: An updated deep learning classifier</li> <li>Explainability of the GWSkyNet-Multi classifier</li> <li>Reconstructing GWs from Supernovae</li> </ul>"},{"location":"about/","title":"Introduction","text":"<p>I completed my BSc in physics and astronomy at the University of British Columbia, Vancouver in 2019, followed by an MSc in astronomy in 2021 supervised by Prof. Jess McIver. My masters thesis focussed on studying gravitational-waves from core-collapse supernovae. In 2022 I moved to Montreal for my PhD in physics at McGill University with Prof. Daryl Haggard. My work focuses on leveraging machine learning techniques to enable multi-messenger observations of gravitational-wave events.</p>"},{"location":"about/#cv","title":"CV","text":""},{"location":"projects/","title":"Projects","text":"<p>I am currently working on the GWSkyNet-Multi project.</p> <p></p>"},{"location":"projects/GWSkyNet-Multi/","title":"GWSkyNet-Multi","text":"<p>GWSkyNet-Multi is a multi-class neural network model developed for the classification of candidate gravitational-wave events detected by the LIGO and Virgo observatories.</p> <p>The model uses limited information released in the low-latency public alerts to produce prediction probabilities indicating whether an event is a merger of two black holes (BBH), a merger of a neutron star and a black hole (NSBH), a merger of two neutron stars (BNS), or a non-astrophysical detector noise transient known as a glitch. This classification facilitates time-sensitive decisions about whether to perform electromagnetic follow-up of candidate events during LIGO-Virgo-KAGRA (LVK) observing runs.</p> <p>Details of the latest GWSkyNet-Multi II model can be found in Raza et al. (2025). The model, associated scripts, and instructions on how to use them can be found in the repository here. Details of earlier versions of the model and its inner workings are described in Abbott et al. (2022) and Raza et al. (2024).</p>"},{"location":"projects/GWSkyNet-Multi/#o4-predictions","title":"O4 Predictions","text":"<p>The model predictions for all significant multi-detector CBC candidate events since the start of the fourth observing run (O4) can be found in the table below. For each candidate event the GWSkyNet-Multi II predicted probability for each class (and the associated uncertainty) is provided, along with a classification label based on the highest probability, and the inputs to the model determined from the BAYESTAR localization.</p> <p>A csv file for the predictions table, which includes predictions for sub-threshold events, can be found here.</p> Event ID Significant Glitch Prob (%) BBH Prob (%) NSBH Prob (%) BN